{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261124T234435Z-e6b3faf9", "ts": "2026-11-24T23:51:48Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.9.1-dev", "branch": "feature/test-determinism", "build": "60ca8d932623"}, "body": {"base_commit": "60ca8d932623ed2d3c41e3f18dd65c3b202ce311", "base_tree": "c8337dd57eb6b78eca49fc8f518726e438f2d460", "head_tree": "b24519c7303d8b1dc80c0e95b355999f881dc21c", "head_commit": "276ed9b5e02a4be97699d0fbdf46d3f227188fa5", "dispatch_commit": "276ed9b5e02a4be97699d0fbdf46d3f227188fa5", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/discipline.md", "plugin/docs/test-determinism.md", "plugin/docs/waivers.md", "plugin/lib/determinism_lint.py", "plugin/lib/record_lint.py", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/skills/critic/goals-1-3.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-protocol.md", "plugin/skills/janitor/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/janitor/themes.md", "plugin/templates/test-specifications.md", "tests/test_critic_consolidate.py", "tests/test_determinism_lint.py", "tests/test_discipline_table.py", "tests/test_hook_argument_shape.py", "tests/test_record_lint.py", "tests/test_reviewer_payload_budget.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-test-determinism.md", ".prawduct/changes/test-determinism.md", ".prawduct/cross-cutting-concerns.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/discipline.md", "plugin/docs/test-determinism.md", "plugin/docs/waivers.md", "plugin/lib/determinism_lint.py", "plugin/lib/record_lint.py", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/skills/critic/goals-1-3.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-protocol.md", "plugin/skills/janitor/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/janitor/themes.md", "plugin/templates/test-specifications.md", "tests/test_critic_consolidate.py", "tests/test_determinism_lint.py", "tests/test_discipline_table.py", "tests/test_hook_argument_shape.py", "tests/test_record_lint.py", "tests/test_reviewer_payload_budget.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-test-determinism.md", ".prawduct/changes/test-determinism.md", ".prawduct/cross-cutting-concerns.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "severity": "blocking", "title": "Governing-artifact disposition false of the diff: an unknown-suffix test file is silently passed, not reported unchecked", "recommendation": "The plan's architecture disposition reads 'written in Python, never specific to Python -> conforms; ... a changed test file in a language with no rules is reported `unchecked`, never silently passed'. architecture.md's Direction interim rule says the same thing: a file prawduct classifies itself 'dispatches from a per-suffix table and reports an unknown suffix as **unchecked** rather than passing it'. The cross-cutting registry adds 'Adopt exclusion-by-default for any new classifier, and treat an allowlist as the departure that needs a reason'. `determinism_lint._subject` does the opposite. It reports `unchecked` only for suffixes in the allowlist `_OTHER_LANGUAGE_SUFFIXES`, and every other suffix returns '', which is silently passed both in review (`check`) and in `scan_tree`'s `unruled`. A test file in an unlisted language therefore yields the same output as one that passed: `web/cart.test.mts`, `foo_test.cxx`, `foo_test.hpp`, `FooTest.kts`, `test_foo.R`, `foo_spec.jl`, `Foo.test.vue`, `.bats`/`.sh` suites. Nothing in the plan's Decisions made mid-build records this departure, so the `conforms` stands unsupported. Why it broke: the classifier lists the languages it knows are unruled instead of excluding what is known not to be source. Close it with a construction, not a longer list. Under a test tree, report every file that is not `.py` and not a known non-source suffix (data, fixtures, snapshots, docs) as `unchecked` under `test-scope`. `test-scope` is already an observation, so this costs no gate. Pin it with a fixture whose suffix appears in no table. Alternatively, record a vetoable decision for the allowlist and correct the disposition. Scope: class.", "files": ["plugin/lib/determinism_lint.py"], "scope": "class", "closure": "A changed or tracked file under a test tree whose suffix is in neither the Python rule set nor `_OTHER_LANGUAGE_SUFFIXES` and is source code.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "plugin/lib/determinism_lint.py:_subject (review path, check())"}, {"id": "m2", "site": "plugin/lib/determinism_lint.py:scan_tree unruled listing"}], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "Ordinal clock-read keys report the pre-existing line when a new read is inserted above an old one, and a waiver there lets the new read pass", "recommendation": "`test-clock-fake` and `test-clock-import` key a site by `(path[, qualname], api, n)`, where n is its ordinal in file order. Base and head are matched by key, so when a change inserts a new `time.time()` ABOVE an existing one in the same function, or anywhere above an existing import-time read, the new read takes n=1 and matches the base's n=1. The reported 'introduced' site is then n=2, which is the OLD line. The builder is pointed at a line the diff never touched. If they follow the finding's own remedy and waive that line, `_unwaived` drops it, the head's single remaining read (the new one) matches the base's n=1, and the introduced read ships with no result at all. The existing test covers only the waived-base variant (`test_a_new_read_does_not_hide_behind_an_old_waived_one`), not an insert-above with no waiver in the base. Fix: among head sites sharing a key stem, attribute the surplus to sites on lines the diff added. record_lint already computes added lines with `git diff --unified=0`. Then pin insert-above for both checks, seen red on the current matcher. Scope: instance (the matcher in `check`).", "files": ["plugin/lib/determinism_lint.py", "tests/test_determinism_lint.py"], "scope": "instance", "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "A trailing waiver on one line also waives the next line's site, so a new import beside a waived one is silently passed", "recommendation": "`_unwaived` uses `waivers.waives(lines, line-1, ...)`, which honours a waiver on the site's line OR the line above. That 'line above' is meant for leading placement, a comment-only line before the site. For code it also matches a TRAILING waiver on the previous statement. Take a root conftest whose line 1 is `import myapp  # prawduct:allow prawduct/test-scope -- plugin registration`. A change adding `import myapp.db` on line 2 produces no `test-scope-conftest` result: a BLOCKING check fails open on exactly the adjacency import blocks always have. The scan_tree test happens to put the waived import below the unwaived one, so it never exercises this. Fix: for these code sites, accept the line above only when it is a comment-only line, and pin it with the adjacent-import fixture seen red. Scope: instance. `compliance.py`'s broad-except shares the matcher but rarely meets adjacent sites. Search `waivers.waives` callers on code if you decide it is a class.", "files": ["plugin/lib/determinism_lint.py"], "scope": "instance", "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "First-party detection admits packages of a project root nested inside a test directory, contrary to the stated rule", "recommendation": "Plan, Detection rules: first-party means a package 'outside any directory named tests/test'. `docs/test-determinism.md`, which consumers read, says '... or under its `src/`, outside a test directory'. Chunk 03's project-roots change broke this. `_project_roots` makes every directory holding a `pyproject.toml`/`setup.py`/`setup.cfg` a root, including fixture projects such as `tests/fixtures/sample/pyproject.toml`. `_importable` then strips that root, so the fixture's packages and modules join `_first_party`, because `_NOT_FIRST_PARTY` filters names only. The consequences are BLOCKING false positives. `_package_of_init` treats a fixture package's `__init__.py` as first-party, so a fixture whose `__init__` imports its three or more submodules (common in import-graph or build-tool products) blocks at the boundary. A root-conftest import of any name a fixture project happens to define (a vendored `yaml`, an `app`) also becomes `test-scope-conftest`. Fix: skip any project root with a test-directory component (`is_test_path`'s directory rule). Pin a fixture project under `tests/` as not first-party, seen red. Scope: instance (`_project_roots`/`_first_party`).", "files": ["plugin/lib/determinism_lint.py", "plugin/docs/test-determinism.md"], "scope": "instance", "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "Python product modules named `*_spec.py` (or under `spec/`/`specs/`) are classified as shared test helpers", "recommendation": "`is_test_path` applies every ecosystem's test-name conventions to `.py` files too: RSpec `_spec`, Jest `.spec`/`.test`, and JVM/.NET `Test`/`Tests`/`Spec` stems, plus `spec`/`specs` directories. pytest collects none of these. A product module such as `myapp/job_spec.py` or `myapp/openapi_spec.py` is therefore a 'test path', and `_is_test_module` is false for it, so every new `datetime.now()`/`uuid4()` in its product functions becomes `test-clock-fake` ('shared test helper `build` reads ...'). Its module-level reads become `test-clock-import`. Ordinary product timestamps turn into WARNINGs the product can clear only by waiving correct code. Why it broke: one path predicate serves both the Python rules and other-language co-location. Fix: for `.py` files, keep the directory rule and treat as test-shaped only `test_*.py`, `*_test.py` and `conftest.py`; leave the cross-language stems for `_subject`'s 'other' branch. Pin `myapp/job_spec.py` as not a test path. Scope: class.", "files": ["plugin/lib/determinism_lint.py"], "scope": "class", "closure": "A `.py` path that `is_test_path` classifies as a test by a naming or directory convention pytest does not use.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "is_test_path: `_spec` / `.spec` / `.test` stem suffixes on .py"}, {"id": "m2", "site": "is_test_path: CamelCase `Test`/`Tests`/`Spec` stems on .py"}, {"id": "m3", "site": "is_test_path: `spec`/`specs` directory names on .py"}], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "Chunk 02 acceptance criterion 'the fresh-agent read changes what the agent writes' has no recorded evidence", "recommendation": "Chunk 02's last acceptance criterion and the plan's Verification Strategy require a fresh agent to read `building.md` \u00a7 Test Discipline, write a fake with a timestamp, and show that the pointer changes what it writes. These are the only checks that the prose instruction has any effect (tests.md: 'When the deliverable is INSTRUCTIONS, at least one guardrail must model the READER'). No commit message, plan decision, change record or owner ruling in the bundle records that it ran or what it showed. The budget and anchor tests pass whether or not the sentence works. Either record the trial's result under Decisions made mid-build, or descope the criterion explicitly. Scope: instance.", "files": ["plugin/methodology/building.md"], "scope": "instance", "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "Chunk 02 planned 'without raising a ceiling'; eight ceilings were raised and the plan does not record the departure", "recommendation": "Chunk 02 Tests: 'the existing budget and discipline-table tests stay green without raising a ceiling.' The diff raises the token ceilings of `review-protocol.md` (4111->4172) and `goals-1-3.md` (2523->2584), and all six reviewer-payload routes in `test_reviewer_payload_budget.py` (+61 each). That puts about 61 tokens on every review dispatch. The test comments declare each raise with a reason, but the plan's governed_by disposition 'goals and verification bind, method is advice -> conforms' promises that a different route is 'recorded under Decisions made mid-build', and the plan still reads as if its no-raise criterion held. Fix: pay the 61 tokens in place in the two Critic files, as building.md was paid, or record the raise and its reason under Decisions made mid-build. Scope: instance.", "files": ["tests/test_reviewer_payload_budget.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py", "plugin/skills/critic/review-protocol.md", "plugin/skills/critic/goals-1-3.md"], "scope": "instance", "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "test-scope-autouse blocks an autouse fixture in a test module, where it widens nothing", "recommendation": "Class. Why it broke: the P2a rule keys on 'an autouse fixture imports first-party code' in any test-tree file, but the cost it blocks for (every test's closure widens) only holds where the fixture reaches beyond the module that declares it, which means a conftest. In `tests/test_a.py` an autouse fixture's lazy `from myapp import core` reaches only that module's tests, and those tests nearly always import first-party code at module level already. The detector passes a module-level `import myapp.core` in the same file, because P1 applies only to root conftests, so it blocks the narrower, lazy form of an import it lets through in the broader form. `TestAutouseAndInit.test_an_importing_autouse_fixture_is_reported_and_an_opt_in_one_is_not` pins that false positive as the intended behaviour. A boundary BLOCKING that a product meets on ordinary test-module fixtures teaches it to waive the check. The shipped doc also disagrees with the code: `docs/test-determinism.md`'s P2 row says the fixture 'imports everything', and its 'What the detector reads' bullets define P2's `__init__` threshold but no autouse rule. Fix the classification rather than the severity: restrict AUTOUSE_CHECK to fixtures declared in a `conftest.py` (`_parts(path)[-1] == \"conftest.py\"`), move the test fixture into `tests/conftest.py`, add a must-not-fire case for a test module's autouse fixture, and state the conftest-only rule in the doc's detector bullets.", "files": ["plugin/lib/determinism_lint.py", "tests/test_determinism_lint.py", "plugin/docs/test-determinism.md"], "scope": "instance", "closure": "The AUTOUSE_CHECK arm of python_sites and the test and doc sentence that describe it.", "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "Shallow clock reads the detector cannot see belong to neither the detector nor the reviewer", "recommendation": "Class. Why it broke: the reviewer's share of P3 is defined by enumerating a residue ('a clock or randomness read made calls deep' in the Goal 1 bullet; 'what a static read cannot: a P3 read made several calls deep' in the doc), not by its relation to the detector, so any P3 shape the detector misses but a static read could see belongs to nobody. A concrete member: the most idiomatic Python fake, `@dataclass class FakeOrder: created: datetime = field(default_factory=datetime.now)` in `tests/fakes.py`. P3 walks only function bodies (`_enclosing_functions`). P4 sees the class body but matches only Calls (`_ambient_name` needs an `ast.Call`), and `datetime.now` here is passed as a reference. A `lambda: datetime.now()` factory is skipped explicitly by `_calls_outside_functions`. So the detector is silent, and a reviewer told that the detector owns shallow reads, and not to re-derive its results, skips it. The doc's claim that the reviewer gets what 'a static read cannot' see is also false of this shape. The doc's own P3 example is 'a fake's constructor calling `datetime.now`'. Close it by construction, not by listing shapes: word the reviewer's share relationally in both carriers and in the doc ('a clock or randomness read in a fake, helper or conftest that the detector did not report'), which stays true whatever the detector learns. Optionally also teach P3 to flag an AMBIENT_CALLS name loaded as a value (a reference, not a call) in a fake's class body and function bodies.", "files": ["plugin/skills/critic/goals-1-3.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-protocol.md", "plugin/docs/test-determinism.md", "plugin/lib/determinism_lint.py"], "scope": "class", "closure": "A static-visible ambient read in a test helper, fake or conftest that python_sites does not report and the reviewer prose does not assign to the reviewer.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "dataclass field(default_factory=<ambient api>) in a fake's class body"}, {"id": "m2", "site": "lambda wrapping an ambient call inside a helper function (skipped by _calls_outside_functions)"}, {"id": "m3", "site": "plugin/skills/critic/goals-1-3.md and review-protocol.md determinism bullet ('made calls deep')"}, {"id": "m4", "site": "plugin/docs/test-determinism.md 'what a static read cannot' sentence"}], "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "First-party detection counts projects nested under a test tree, contrary to the doc", "recommendation": "Instance. Why it broke: `_project_roots` makes every directory holding a `pyproject.toml`/`setup.py`/`setup.cfg` a root, including `tests/fixtures/sample_project/`, and `_first_party` filters only top-level names in `_NOT_FIRST_PARTY`. `docs/test-determinism.md` says first-party code is found 'outside a test directory'. The consequence is a BLOCKING one: in a Python dev-tool product, which keeps sample projects under its test fixtures, `tests/fixtures/sample_project/pkg/__init__.py` importing its three submodules is a first-party P2b `test-scope-init`. An edit to that fixture then blocks the boundary review, and it can only be waived. Fix the code to match the doc: drop any root that `is_test_path` places inside a test tree (test a root's directory parts against `_TEST_DIRS`). Add a must-not-fire fixture for a nested sample project's import-everything `__init__`.", "files": ["plugin/lib/determinism_lint.py", "plugin/docs/test-determinism.md"], "scope": "instance", "closure": "_project_roots/_first_party accepting a root inside a test tree.", "fid": "R-10"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "Inferred P2a rule blocks on any single first-party import from an autouse fixture, but the owner's P2 and the shipped doc both say 'imports everything'", "recommendation": "The plan's Governance Checkpoints ask this boundary review to judge the 'Detection rules (Inferred \u2014 mine, not the owner's)' against the owner's goals. P1, P2b, P3 and P4 hold up against them. P2a does not. The owner ruled BLOCKING for 'P2 (an autouse fixture or package `__init__` that imports everything)' (owner-rulings.md, 2026-11-24, item 2), and plugin/docs/test-determinism.md's P2 row repeats 'An always-on fixture (pytest: autouse=True) imports everything'. The detector, though, emits blocking `test-scope-autouse` for an autouse fixture whose body imports one first-party module (python_sites: any `first_party_of(module)` hit inside an `_is_autouse_fixture` body). Your P2b reading takes 'everything' literally (all submodules, at least three), so the two halves of one owner sentence get opposite readings. The consequence: a governed product adding the common `autouse` reset fixture (`from myapp.db import reset_db`) hits a merge blocker that the doc it is pointed to says it should not. That blocker can only be cleared with a waiver or a fix round, and both are full-price at a boundary. Instance fix, one of two: (a) get the owner to confirm that any first-party import from an autouse fixture counts as P2, record it where the plan's goals live, and make the doc's P2 row and its 'What the review detects' bullet state the autouse threshold beside the `__init__` one; or (b) narrow the rule to what the owner ruled. Either way, update the plan's Inferred marker.", "files": ["plugin/lib/determinism_lint.py", "plugin/docs/test-determinism.md"], "scope": "instance", "closure": "The one inferred detection rule whose blocking threshold is broader than the owner-ruled wording and the shipped catalogue row it maps to.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "plugin/lib/determinism_lint.py python_sites AUTOUSE_CHECK branch"}], "fid": "R-11"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "Shipped doc's 'Measured evidence' section describes a review comparison that does not exist", "recommendation": "plugin/docs/test-determinism.md ends with: 'When a runner reports each test's determinism causes, a later change lets the review compare that list with the base's, so a cause site the diff introduced ... is a finding under the same check ids above.' Nothing in plugin/ reads per-test causes; a grep for the channel finds only this sentence, and the measured leg is the follow-up #1244, which is blocked on #1096. A consumer reads 'a later change' as their own next change, so they will wire the runner's causes and expect the review to flag cause sites it never compares. That is a reader relying on a check that does not exist. The plan asked for 'a forward reference rather than a claim', and this wording reads as a claim. Instance fix: delete the section until #1244 lands, or make it relational ('prawduct does not yet read a runner's per-test causes'). Don't reword the narration.", "files": ["plugin/docs/test-determinism.md"], "scope": "instance", "closure": "A shipped-doc sentence stating a review capability that no code implements.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "plugin/docs/test-determinism.md \u00a7 Measured evidence"}], "fid": "R-12"}], "counts": {"blocking": 1, "warning": 11, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "Detector's git listing is cwd-relative, so a project nested in a subdirectory of its repo would pass silently", "recommendation": "`_listing` runs `git ls-tree -r --name-only <tree>` from project_dir without `--full-tree`. In a subdirectory, git limits the listing to that directory and prints cwd-relative names. `files_changed` (from `evidence.tree_diff`) is repo-root-relative, and `cat-file <tree>:<path>` resolves from the tree root, so `check()` would match nothing and return a clean ([], [], set()). `scan_tree` would read no blobs and still report 'N scanned', a false clean. `evidence.py` passes `--full-tree` for this reason, and consolidation's `--show-prefix` handling implies such layouts exist. If they are supported, add `--full-tree` and a subdirectory fixture. Otherwise nothing is needed.", "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "determinism-scan returns 1 on a lib ImportError, where the contract lists 0/2/3", "recommendation": "`cmd_determinism_scan`'s ImportError branch returns 1. api-contract.md and the plan disposition say 'exit 0 ... 2 on an unknown argument; 3 when HEAD could not be read'. It is the same shape `stale-plugin-caches` uses, and only a broken install reaches it. Return 3 (could not run) or list 1 in the contract.", "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "Chunk 03 criterion 'Nothing is written into the product' has no test", "recommendation": "Reading the code shows it holds: git reads only, and an ephemeral-safe registration. But no test asserts a clean `git status --porcelain` and no new paths after `determinism-scan`, so a later write would ship green.", "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "P3 misses some common fake shapes (reviewer backstop applies)", "recommendation": "`_enclosing_functions` walks only functions and classes directly in module or class bodies, so a helper defined under a module-level `if`/`try` is never checked. A dataclass fake's `field(default_factory=datetime.now)` / `uuid.uuid4` is a reference, not a call, so it is never seen. The doc's 'reviewer judges ... a P3 read made several calls deep' does not cover either, so they fall to the reviewer's general Goal 1 bullet.", "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "The hypothesis remedy-effect test disables the database rather than relocating it", "recommendation": "`test_following_the_remedy_clears_it` uses `database=None`, and the detector clears on the substring `database=` anywhere in a conftest or config file. The test therefore pins marker presence, not the advice's effect ('point it outside the checkout'). `database=DirectoryBasedExampleDatabase('.hypothesis')` would also clear it. It is advice only, so no edit is required.", "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "read-cost: whole-tree scan rescans the full listing once per package __init__", "recommendation": "In `scan_tree`, every `__init__.py` calls `_package_of_init`, which calls `_project_roots(listing)` again, a full pass over the listing, and then `_siblings(directory, listing)`, another full pass. The cost is O(inits x listing) even though `_first_party` already computed the roots once. This is janitor-only, run on demand, with no stated volume, so it is an observation. If it is ever touched, compute the roots once and index the listing by directory, then pass both into python_sites.", "files": ["plugin/lib/determinism_lint.py"], "check": "read-cost", "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "Ordinal site keys duplicate the Counter multiset match", "recommendation": "`check()` already matches base and head sites as a multiset (`Counter`), so the per-(place, api) ordinal that `python_sites` appends to clock-site keys adds nothing, and neither does the renumbering `_unwaived` repeats for CLOCK_FAKE_CHECK and CLOCK_IMPORT_CHECK by name. Without ordinals, head reads beyond the base's count are reported in the same order. The two counters must agree, and a future ordinal-keyed check that `_unwaived` does not list would pair across waivers. Correct today and local, so no edit is owed.", "files": ["plugin/lib/determinism_lint.py"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "lint_records docstring still says it runs over the record subset", "recommendation": "`lint_records`'s docstring opens 'Run every check over the record subset of `paths`'. The new test-scope arm runs over all changed paths, which the in-body comment states. This is docstring wording that nothing reads, so it is an observation. Make it relational ('every check; the record checks read the record subset') if the function is touched again.", "files": ["plugin/lib/record_lint.py"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "P1-P8 pattern numbers share the Pn spelling shipped docs use for principles", "recommendation": "`docs/test-determinism.md` numbers patterns P1-P8, while `docs/waivers.md`'s new `test-scope` row cites 'P1 Tests Are Contracts', meaning principle 1, in the same breath as test-scope sites. Inside each doc the context disambiguates, and no finding text emits the numbers, so this is an observation only.", "files": ["plugin/docs/test-determinism.md", "plugin/docs/waivers.md"], "oid": "O-9"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "An unparseable test-tree .py is an unclearable boundary warning", "recommendation": "A deliberately invalid Python fixture under a test tree, which is common in linters, formatters and codemod tools, is reported `test-scope-conftest unchecked`, a warning at the boundary, on every change that touches it. No waiver can reach it, because the file never parses. This was a recorded decision ('authority fails closed'), so it is only noted. If a product meets it, honouring the `prawduct/test-scope` waiver on an unparseable file's first line would let it clear deliberately.", "files": ["plugin/lib/determinism_lint.py"], "oid": "O-10"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Records Pass: coverage and result", "recommendation": "Covered all six files_oracle: api-contract.md, architecture.md, build-plan-test-determinism.md, changes/test-determinism.md, cross-cutting-concerns.md, owner-rulings.md. None clears a bar. The change record's consumer note matches the detector: three import shapes block, existing sites don't, and the waiver id is right. The api-contract exit codes (0/2/3) and JSON keys match scan_cmd and _reject_unknown_args. The architecture and record_lint cost claims match check(), which returns early with no candidates and lists names while reading only changed blobs. owner-rulings records the 'Janitor only' supersession of item 3. Sub-bar notes, no edit wanted: the cross-cutting Testing row's Notes cell runs two sentences together ('...one decision, not two Test determinism's detection leg...'); the plan's Level: Medium still says the route 'isn't verified yet', though Chunk 01's recorded decision settled it; and api-contract does not list determinism-scan's exit 1 on a lib ImportError, which every thin-wrapper command shares.", "oid": "O-11"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "A non-Syntax parse failure in the detector degrades all of record-lint, not just its own arm", "recommendation": "check() catches only SyntaxError/ValueError from ast.parse. A RecursionError on pathologically nested source would go up to lint_records_safe, which turns the whole record_lint result into one unchecked entry, so every other record check on that review would also go unanswered. It is visible, not silent, and the trigger is unlikely, so this is noted only. Catching RecursionError beside SyntaxError would keep it to the arm's own unparsed entry.", "oid": "O-12"}], "duration_seconds": 1000, "scope": "test-determinism", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null